-- title:
--   Image of the origin-chart kernel in T[[X]]
-- statement:
--   Let $T$ be a commutative local ring that is adically complete for its maximal ideal, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $T$, and let $P$ be a section of the projective Weierstrass model of $W$ over $\operatorname{Spec} T$ (a morphism to the model commuting with the structure maps). Let $\chi \colon \mathrm{OriginChartRing}\,W \to T$ be a ring homomorphism, where $\mathrm{OriginChartRing}\,W$ is the degree-zero homogeneous localisation of the graded quotient ring of the projective model away from the coordinate $\mathrm{coord}\,W\,1$, containing the elements $\mathrm{xOverY}\,W$ and $\mathrm{zOverY}\,W$ (the classes of $\mathrm{coord}\,W\,0$ and $\mathrm{coord}\,W\,2$ divided by $\mathrm{coord}\,W\,1$). Assume $\mathrm{ReducesToOrigin}\,P\,\chi\,(\mathfrak m_T)$, that is: $\chi$ describes $P$ on the origin chart, and both $\mathrm{originParam}\,\chi = -\chi(\mathrm{xOverY}\,W)$ and $\mathrm{originW}\,\chi = -\chi(\mathrm{zOverY}\,W)$ lie in the maximal ideal. Let $\Phi \colon \mathrm{OriginChartRing}\,W \to T[\![X]\!]$ be a ring homomorphism which sends the image of a scalar $t \in T$ (through the degree-zero part of the grading) to the constant series $C\,t$, sends $\mathrm{xOverY}\,W$ to $-X$, and sends $\mathrm{zOverY}\,W$ to $-W.\mathrm{formalW}$, the power series whose $n$-th coefficient is the $n$-th coefficient of the $n$-th iterate of the substitution $W.\mathrm{wSubst}$. Then the ideal of $T[\![X]\!]$ generated by $\Phi(\ker \chi)$ is the principal ideal generated by $X - C(\mathrm{originParam}\,\chi)$.
--
--   This identifies the divisor cut out by a section reducing to the origin, read in the formal neighbourhood of the origin: pushing the kernel of the origin-chart evaluation forward along the formal parametrisation gives exactly the ideal of the point $X = \mathrm{originParam}\,\chi$. It is used in the comparison of torsion divisors with kernels of origin charts, and in the factorisation of the division series attached to a Drinfeld basis.
